  "worked_solution": "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.\\,\u2713$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,\u2713$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\pi ,-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.\\,\u2713$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\pi $ lies within this range. $\\,\u2713$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,\\pi )$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061x.$",
